# No Compatibility Layer

When changing or removing something, replace it coherently. Retain a parallel runtime path only
when an explicit migration requirement needs the bounded transition described below.

## Do not add

- compatibility aliases for renamed methods, constants, files, or paths
- unbounded transitional layers that let old and new behavior coexist "for now"
- dead shims or wrappers kept only to avoid updating callers
- default-value fallbacks introduced to tolerate the absence of the new behavior
- legacy route aliases, redirect-only compatibility routes, duplicated namespace paths, or old
  controller names unless explicitly required
- compatibility exports, renamed re-exports, alias functions, fallback components, legacy route
  paths, or transitional loaders/actions in JavaScript or TypeScript

## Replace completely

Prefer complete replacement when compatibility is not explicitly required. Remove old paths,
aliases and unused runtime bridges. Preserve historical evidence and decision provenance; a runtime
rename does not authorize rewriting history. Vendor discovery adapters with one canonical source
are not parallel implementations.

Do not keep old controller inheritance paths as compatibility shims. Leaf controllers must inherit
from the intended current base controller directly.

Do not preserve obsolete model names, concern names, service aliases, constants, scopes, callbacks,
or wrapper names merely to avoid changing call sites.

Do not make TypeScript types permissive to preserve old behavior. Avoid unnecessary unions,
optional fields, `any`, `unknown` pass-throughs, and partial compatibility shapes.

## Replace, then update callers

When you rename or restructure, update every reference in the same change and delete the old
definition. A reference that still points at the old path is a bug to fix, not a reason to keep a
shim.

Tests must assert only the final intended behavior. Do not make tests accept both old and new
behavior unless an explicit compatibility requirement says both are valid.

## Why

Transitional layers accumulate. Each one adds a second code path that must be understood, tested, and
eventually removed — and "eventually" rarely comes. Clean replacement keeps exactly one correct path
and makes the change reviewable.

Removing the old path may surface broken references. That is the intended signal: fix them, do not
hide them behind an alias.

## Required transitions

When safe migration or deployment explicitly requires compatibility, state its owner, consumers,
verification and removal condition. Preserve the root authorization and persistence safeguards.
Update current navigation with moves; retain historical path citations as historical citations.
